One algorithm that can be used to find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array is called "Two Pass Selection Sort". This algorithm works by first sorting the entire array using selection sort, then selecting the second largest element from the sorted array.
Here's a C++ implementation of this algorithm:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n) {
    // First pass: Find the largest element
    int max1 = INT_MIN;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > max1) {
            max1 = arr[i];
        }
    }

    // Second pass: Find the second largest element
    int max2 = INT_MIN;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (i != 0 && arr[i] > max2 && arr[i] < max1) {
            max2 = arr[i];
        }
    }
    return max2;
}
```
This function takes an array `arr` and its size `n` as inputs and returns the second largest integer element. It uses two nested loops to iterate through the array and keep track of the maximum elements seen so far (`max1`) and the second maximum element (`max2`). The outer loop selects the largest element, while the inner loop selects the second largest element. Finally, it returns the value of `max2`.
The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the length of the array. This is because both passes through the array take linear time, and there are no additional operations required outside of those two passes.

